 "title": "Why You Might Be Too Valuable to Promote",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "This video illustrates a scenario where an employee is denied a promotion because they are too valuable in their current role. The creator highlights how exceptional performance and taking on extra work can sometimes trap you in your position if replacing you requires hiring multiple people.",
 "key_points": [
  "Employees can be denied promotions not for lack of qualification, but because they are too valuable in their current position.",
  "If promoting an employee requires hiring two or three people to replace their current output, the company may choose to keep them in place.",
  "Doing above and beyond can sometimes result in staying stuck while doing the bare minimum might leave room for consideration.",
  "Employees facing this situation often have to look elsewhere to advance their careers."
 ],
 "content": "When the company won't promote you because... We are not considering you for the promotion at this time. Okay... Can I ask why? We are looking at other candidates. Am I not qualified? Oh, you are. Okay... Have I not met all of my targets and deadlines in my current role? You have. Have I not gone above and beyond since working here? You absolutely have. You've taken on much more than your initial scope. Right. So I have not earned a promotion or the chance to at least be considered? Am I not a valuable member of this team? You are too valuable. Pardon me? If we promoted you and had to replace you, we would need to hire 2, maybe 3 people to fill what you're doing currently. Too valuable? So, while you would thrive in the promotion, the company sees more value in keeping you in your current role. So if I had done the bare minimum well, I may have been considered? Potentially, yes. So now if I ever want to move up in my career, I'll have to do that elsewhere? Now, hold up. Let's, let's not do anything crazy. Understood. I'll go and touch up my resume. Toodaloo.",
